index 32c07eca..677f5fdb 100644
--- a/packages/client/ui-conversation/README.md
+++ b/packages/client/ui-conversation/README.md
@@ -144,6 +144,23 @@ When another writer owns the Session, the send-error toast asks the user to quit
 
 Two independent Escape presses in the focused Chat or Composer stop its current running turn and preserve queued messages. The interval comes from the shortcuts plugin’s `stopSequenceMs` configuration (500 ms by default). A menu, approval, modal, terminal, embedded webpage, composition, repeated key, changed input region, Session or turn breaks the sequence. The shortcut uses the same scoped cancellation as the Stop button. The plugin registers Stop as a fixed action in the `input` display group. Its registration reserves plain Escape against editable shortcuts and supplies the `Esc Esc` sequence shown in the Stop button’s hover and keyboard-focus tooltip.
 
+<a id="provider-owned-semantic-drafts"></a>
+## Provider-owned semantic drafts
+
+`draftPersistence: 'provider'` opts into `conversation.input.registerDraftProvider(ctx, provider)`. The default is `'stock'`: existing SessionId storage and submission behavior remain unchanged. Provider mode bypasses every shared Conversation localStorage read and write, including view preferences. Registration is exclusive and effect-owned; the provider owns durable storage and authorization, while Conversation owns the editor and transient upload handles.
+
+`DraftScope` names the tab owner, persistent Host context, source, native Session, projection generation and storage key with distinct opaque branded types. Public constructors validate nonempty identities without imposing a native format. A storage key isolates tab/source/native Session and survives refresh or Host restart; a process or projection generation belongs only in the live scope. Decode durable records with `parseSemanticDraftRecord`; unknown fields and receipt-bearing originals are rejected. Ordered logical original references remain separate from runtime readiness, receipts and URLs.
+
+`draft(binding)` exposes a stable observable semantic snapshot. `restoreDraft(binding, signal?)` reads through the provider; `hydrateDraft({binding, scope, expectedRevision, record, signal?})` atomically restores supplied text, references, quotes, command metadata and ordered originals. The exact retained binding, full live scope and hydration revision are checked after asynchronous work. Newer edits, submissions, replacement restores, cancellation and scope disposal reject stale publication. Editable restoration reauthorizes original bytes and creates fresh runtime uploads; automatic hydration does not renew retention.
+
+Before any provider-mode command adjudication or Remote execution, Conversation freezes an `unresolved` snapshot and awaits `provider.write(..., 'submission', signal)`. Writes are serialized. The provider must resolve only after durable publication and reject failures or cancelled publication; failure never authorizes execution. An unread unresolved record cannot be replaced by an ordinary edit. The marker contains a local recovery correlation ID and the attempted semantic document/original references; it is not a native receipt or another transcript.
+
+`beforeDispatch(scope, { submissionId, requestId }, signal)` optionally durably associates the protected marker with the exact public DSH prompt request ID immediately before dispatch, including empty continuation prompts. The hook is awaited and its failure or cancellation blocks dispatch. The IDs do not establish native acceptance or durable entry commitment; authoritative reconciliation belongs to the native integration. Providers must check the supplied cancellation signal and their storage compare-and-swap authority at publication, so delayed writes cannot replace a newer owner or scope.
+
+Refresh restores an unresolved record in a read-only sending phase without uploading originals or submitting anything. Transport failure, acceptance, provider withdrawal and refresh do not clear its marker. The integrating provider obtains an authoritative native outcome and explicitly calls `resolveDraftSubmission` with the current binding, full scope, revision, marker ID and `accepted` or `not-submitted`. Resolution persists before unlocking; stale, cancelled or failed resolution stays frozen. Accepted recovery removes only the attempted input and preserves a newer draft using explicit `ownsDraft` metadata, without matching text. Not-submitted recovery refuses to replace a separate newer draft; its owner must retain both snapshots for an explicit recovery choice. Not-submitted recovery returns the protected input for a later explicit send; original readiness remains invalid until an explicit restore obtains fresh bytes. No recovery decision automatically sends.
+
+Withdrawal aborts provider work, releases its runtime attachment handles, preserves text and original references, and invalidates readiness. A replacement registration retains observable identity. Consumers must provide recovery UI and an authoritative outcome lookup; neither a lost reply nor an absent native receipt establishes that an attempt was not submitted.
+
